      "text": "To solve for all integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) has at least one integer root, we can use the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed as a fraction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), must have \\(p\\) as a factor of the constant term (7) and \\(q\\) as a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible rational roots are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7\\).\n\nWe will test each of these possible roots to see which ones are actually roots of the polynomial.\n\n1. Testing \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[\n1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + a \\cdot 1 + 7 = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 11 + a\n\\]\nFor \\(x = 1\\) to be a root, we need:\n\\[\n11 + a = 0 \\implies a = -11\n\\]\nSo, \\(a = -11\\) is a solution.\n\n2. Testing \\(x = -1\\):\n\\[\n(-1)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-1)^2 + a \\cdot (-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n\\]\nFor \\(x = -1\\) to be a root, we need:\n\\[\n9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9\n\\]\nSo, \\(a = 9\\) is a solution.\n\n3. Testing \\(x = 7\\):\n\\[\n7^3 + 3 \\cdot 7^2 + a \\cdot 7 + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n\\]\nFor \\(x = 7\\) to be a root, we need:\n\\[\n497 + 7a = 0 \\implies a = -\\frac{497}{7} = -71\n\\]\nSo, \\(a = -71\\) is a solution.\n\n4. Testing \\(x = -7\\):\n\\[\n(-7)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-7)^2 + a \\cdot (-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -189 - 7a\n\\]\nFor \\(x = -7\\) to be a root, we need:\n\\[\n-189 - 7a = 0 \\implies a = -\\frac{189}{7} = -27\n\\]\nSo, \\(a = -27\\) is a solution.\n\nThe integer values of \\(a\\) that satisfy the condition are \\(-11, 9, -71, -27\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71, -27}\n\\]",
