      "text": "We start by letting \\( r \\) be the integer root of the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\). By the Rational Root Theorem, the possible integer roots are the factors of the constant term (7) divided by the factors of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible integer roots are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 7 \\).\n\nWe need to check each of these possible roots to see if they are indeed roots of the polynomial.\n\n1. **Check \\( r = 1 \\):**\n   \\[\n   1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + a \\cdot 1 + 7 = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 11 + a\n   \\]\n   For \\( r = 1 \\) to be a root, we need:\n   \\[\n   11 + a = 0 \\implies a = -11\n   \\]\n\n2. **Check \\( r = -1 \\):**\n   \\[\n   (-1)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-1)^2 + a \\cdot (-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n   \\]\n   For \\( r = -1 \\) to be a root, we need:\n   \\[\n   9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Check \\( r = 7 \\):**\n   \\[\n   7^3 + 3 \\cdot 7^2 + a \\cdot 7 + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\( r = 7 \\) to be a root, we need:\n   \\[\n   497 + 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -497 \\implies a = -71\n   \\]\n\n4. **Check \\( r = -7 \\):**\n   \\[\n   (-7)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-7)^2 + a \\cdot (-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -199 - 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\( r = -7 \\) to be a root, we need:\n   \\[\n   -199 - 7a = 0 \\implies -7a = 199 \\implies a = -\\frac{199}{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\( a \\) must be an integer, \\( r = -7 \\) is not a root.\n\nFrom the above calculations, the integer values of \\( a \\) that make \\( x = 1 \\), \\( x = -1 \\), or \\( x = 7 \\) a root of the polynomial are \\( a = -11 \\), \\( a = 9 \\), and \\( a = -71 \\).\n\nThus, the possible integer values of \\( a \\) are \\(\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\\).",
